SPARC has genuinely strong empirical results and a well-specified method with good ablations, but its two central claims are overstated: the "6% of parameters" figure conflates architectural and methodological savings, and Class-IL inference cost scaling is unacknowledged. Relative to the anchors, SPARC sits above the 4.25–5.0 range (stronger results, better ablations than those papers) but below the 7.5 range (less novel, no theory, overclaiming). The overclaiming is a meaningful issue but doesn't invalidate the real contributions — the method works well and the results are solid when read with appropriate caveats. A score of 5.5 reflects a paper with real merit but significant framing issues that should be corrected.

