### Harsh Critic
# Review of FSL-MIC: An Attentional Few-Shot Learning Framework for EEG Motor Imagery Classification

## Critical Issues

### 1. Structural: The headline claim of "significantly outperforms traditional methods" is contradicted by the paper's own results.

The abstract states "The proposed FSL framework significantly outperforms traditional methods," but the only fair comparison — between RelationNet-attention and CNN-attention-Few (both using comparable numbers of labeled samples from the test subject) — shows marginal and inconsistent differences. On BCI 2a, RelationNet at 20-shot (72.6%) outperforms CNN-attention-Few (62.8%) by ~10 points. On BCI 2b, the gap is only ~2 points (73.2% vs. 71.3%), well within the standard deviations (±6.1 vs. ±6.1). On the Experimental dataset, RelationNet at 20-shot **underperforms** CNN-attention-Few (68.2% vs. 69.2%). At lower shots, RelationNet consistently underperforms CNN-attention-Few on 2/3 datasets. The comparison with CNN-attention-All (trained on all data) is irrelevant to the FSL claim — of course a model with full data access outperforms one with limited data. The paper's framing misrepresents these results by grouping the All model into the same table and then claiming FSL superiority, when the actual evidence for FSL advantage over the appropriate baseline is weak at best.

### 2. Structural: No comparison with existing FSL methods for EEG MI classification.

The paper cites An et al. (2020, 2023) as direct prior work applying FSL to the same BCI 2a and 2b datasets, yet provides no numerical comparison with their results. Section 4.4 mentions differences in approach (DA vs. fine-tuning, different data splits) but never presents a head-to-head accuracy comparison. Without this, the paper cannot establish that it advances over existing FSL methods for this task. This is not a minor gap — it is the absence of the most important comparison, since the paper's contribution is specifically an FSL method for EEG MI.

### 3. Evidential: The attention module's contribution is entirely unsubstantiated.

The attention mechanism is presented as a core contribution and one of the three "interconnected modules" of the framework. Yet there is no ablation comparing RelationNet with vs. without attention. There is no comparison with a vanilla Relation Network (Sung et al., 2018). The attention heatmaps are deferred to supplementary material and limited to a single subject ("broader results involving multiple subjects will be presented in subsequent work," Section 3.2). Without an ablation, the claim that attention "improves prediction accuracy" (Section 3.2) is unsupported — the improvement over baselines could be entirely attributable to the RelationNet structure itself.

## Section-by-Section Notes

**Abstract:** Claims "significantly outperforms traditional methods" — see Critical Issue 1.

**Section 1 (Introduction):** The contributions listed are vague. "Introducing a class of architectures for EEG meta-learning" overstates what is essentially one architecture (a Relation Network with attention). "Combines temporal convolutions and attention mechanisms to reduce training time" — training time reduction is never measured or reported.

**Section 3.2 (Self-Attention):** The attention formula uses $S = QK^T$ without the standard $1/\sqrt{d}$ scaling factor from Vaswani (2017), which the paper cites. The subsequent $\tanh(M)$ is also non-standard and unexplained. These deviations from the cited architecture are not justified. The section also claims interpretability but defers all evidence to supplementary material and future work, making the claim unverified in the main paper.

**Section 3.3 (Relation Module):** Focal loss is introduced with parameters $\alpha$ and $\gamma$, but their values are never specified. The choice of focal loss over standard cross-entropy is never justified. Kernel sizes (30×1, 15×1) and FC dimensions (256, 100) are presented without any sensitivity analysis.

**Section 4.1 (Dataset):** The experimental dataset has only 7 subjects — extremely small for cross-subject evaluation with 7-fold CV. Each fold tests on a single subject, meaning results are dominated by individual subject variability.

**Section 4.2 (Experimental Setting):** The evaluation protocol is confusingly described. It states "each training set included all experiments from a subject except the last one, which served as the validation set" — this suggests session-level splitting within subjects, but the cross-validation is described as subject-level (train on N-1 subjects, test on 1). These two descriptions are contradictory. Additionally, for CNN-attention-Few, it is unclear whether the model is trained from scratch on only 40 samples or fine-tuned from a pre-trained model — this critically affects the fairness of the comparison.

**Section 4.3 (Results):** The paper mentions data augmentation (DA) as a key technique and reports "DA Accuracy" as a separate metric, but never explains what DA technique is applied, when it is applied (training only? training and testing?), or how it differs per dataset. The term "DA accuracy" appears in tables without definition. The discussion of BCI 2a results incorrectly attributes difficulty to "22 channels" rather than the well-known issue of inter-subject variability and no feedback.

**Section 4.4 (Conclusion):** Claims the model "outperforms" An et al. without presenting any comparative numbers. Also claims the architecture "is not restricted to EEG data alone" without any evidence from other domains.

## Strengths

- The problem motivation is sound: reducing calibration time for new BCI users via few-shot learning is practically important.
- The paper evaluates on three datasets including a newly collected one with 64 channels, and the data is made available on Figshare.
- The cross-subject leave-one-out evaluation protocol is the appropriate protocol for the claimed use case (generalizing to unseen subjects).

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Ablation of the attention module** — without RelationNet-without-attention results, the claim that attention improves accuracy is unsubstantiated.
2. **Comparison with An et al. (2020, 2023) on BCI 2a and 2b** — this is the most directly comparable prior work and its absence means the contribution over existing FSL methods for EEG MI cannot be assessed.
3. **Comparison with standard EEG baselines** (e.g., FBCSP, EEGNet) configured in a few-shot setting — the only non-FSL baseline is the authors' own CNN-attention model.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Per-subject breakdown of results** — with 7–9 subjects, reporting only mean±std obscures whether the method works reliably or succeeds on easy subjects and fails on hard ones. This is especially critical given the high standard deviations (e.g., ±7.2% on BCI 2b 1-shot).
2. **Statistical significance testing** — the differences between RelationNet-attention and CNN-attention-Few are often within one standard deviation; paired statistical tests would clarify whether observed differences are meaningful.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Attention heatmaps across multiple subjects** — the paper acknowledges this is deferred to future work, but a single-subject heatmap in supplementary material does not establish that the attention mechanism produces consistent, interpretable patterns.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Specify all hyperparameters** (focal loss α, γ; learning rate decay schedule; number of training episodes) to enable reproducibility.

## Overall Assessment

This paper does not establish its central claims. The headline result — that FSL "significantly outperforms traditional methods" — is not supported by the data, which shows marginal and inconsistent improvements over the only fair baseline (CNN-attention-Few). The most important comparison — against existing FSL methods for EEG MI (An et al.) — is absent. The attention module, presented as a core contribution, has no ablation to justify its inclusion. The evaluation is further weakened by underspecified protocols, missing hyperparameters, and very small subject counts. The paper should not be accepted in its current form; at minimum, it requires ablations, comparison with existing FSL baselines, and a reframing of claims to match what the evidence actually shows.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Clear demonstration that the FSL framework outperforms supervised learning under limited data conditions on the most challenging dataset**: On BCI 2a (no neurofeedback, 22 channels), the RelationNet-attention model with 20 shots achieves 72.6% ± 2.9 accuracy versus CNN-attention-Few's 62.8% ± 4.3—a nearly 10-point improvement using the same number of labeled samples from the test subject (Table 1). This directly supports the claim that FSL-MIC enables better classification of unseen subjects with limited data than naive supervised training.

2. **Learned non-linear relation module replaces fixed distance metrics**: Unlike prior few-shot EEG work by Burrello et al. (2019) that used distance-based prototype vectors, and Narwariya et al. (2020) that used fixed nearest-neighbor classifiers on embeddings, the proposed relation module (Section 3.3) uses CNN + FC layers to learn non-linear comparisons between support and query embeddings. This is a distinct architectural choice that the paper argues improves generalization from limited examples.

3. **Integration of channel-wise self-attention with the relation network for EEG**: The self-attention mechanism (Section 3.2) computes a C×C attention score matrix across electrode channels, allowing the model to learn inter-channel dependencies before relation scoring. This differs from An et al. (2023), which evaluated different embedding modules without an attention-based relation component, and from Lashgari et al. (2021), which used attention in a standard supervised CNN but not in a few-shot setting.

### Supporting strengths

1. **Evaluation across three datasets with systematically varying properties**: The paper evaluates on BCI 2a (22 channels, no feedback), BCI 2b (3 channels, with feedback), and a new 64-channel experimental dataset (Table 1, Figure 3). This covers a useful range of channel counts and feedback conditions, providing evidence that the framework is not tuned to a single data regime.

2. **Practically relevant cross-subject evaluation protocol**: The 9-fold and 7-fold cross-validation (Section 4.2) trains on all but one subject and tests on the held-out subject, which is the challenging and clinically relevant setting for BCI systems where new users cannot provide extensive calibration data.

3. **New 64-channel dataset with EMG monitoring**: The collected experimental dataset (Section 4.1.1) includes 7 participants with bipolar EMG electrodes on the brachioradialis to verify absence of movement during MI trials, adding quality control that strengthens the data's validity and contributes a new resource to the community.

### Presentation / practical strengths

1. **Interpretability through attention score visualization**: Section 3.2 describes how the C×C attention weight matrix can be visualized as a heatmap across electrode channels, identifying which electrodes are most influential for classification—providing neuroscientific interpretability alongside prediction performance.

2. **Use of focal loss for relation scoring**: The adoption of focal loss (Section 3.3, Eq.) rather than standard cross-entropy for the relation module is a practical choice that helps handle class imbalance in the binary matching task (matching vs. non-matching support-query pairs).

## Summary

The paper proposes FSL-MIC, a few-shot learning framework combining a temporal convolution embedding module, a channel-wise self-attention mechanism, and a learned relation module for cross-subject EEG motor imagery classification. The most important piece of evidence supporting this contribution is Table 1, which shows that on BCI 2a—the most challenging dataset without neurofeedback—the 20-shot RelationNet-attention model achieves 72.6% accuracy compared to 62.8% for a supervised CNN trained on the same limited data, demonstrating that the proposed architecture effectively leverages episodic meta-learning to generalize to unseen subjects with minimal calibration data.

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary

The paper proposes FSL-MIC, a few-shot learning framework for EEG motor imagery (MI) classification combining three modules: a temporal convolution embedding module, a channel-wise self-attention mechanism, and a CNN-based relation module with focal loss. The framework is evaluated on BCI Competition IV 2a, 2b, and a newly collected 64-channel dataset, using cross-subject leave-one-out cross-validation with K-shot settings (K=1,5,10,20), comparing against CNN-attention baselines with limited (Few) and full (All) data access.

## Strengths

- **Important and well-motivated problem**: Reducing calibration time for new BCI users via few-shot learning is practically significant. The clinical relevance—especially for patients with disabilities who face fatigue during extended training—is clearly articulated (Section 4.4).

- **Clear demonstration of FSL benefit on the most challenging dataset**: On BCI 2a (no neurofeedback), RelationNet-attention at 20-shot achieves 72.6% ± 2.9 vs. CNN-attention-Few's 62.8% ± 4.3—a meaningful ~10-point improvement using the same number of labeled samples from the test subject (Table 1). This is the paper's strongest result.

- **Evaluation across three datasets with varying properties**: Testing on BCI 2a (22 channels, no feedback), BCI 2b (3 channels, with feedback), and a 64-channel experimental dataset covers a useful range of channel counts and feedback conditions (Section 4.1, Table 1).

- **New 64-channel dataset with EMG monitoring**: The collected experimental dataset (Section 4.1.1) includes EMG electrodes on the brachioradialis to verify absence of movement during MI trials, adding quality control. The data is made available on Figshare.

- **Appropriate evaluation protocol**: Cross-subject leave-one-out cross-validation (Section 4.2) is the right protocol for the claimed use case—generalizing to unseen subjects with minimal calibration data.

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
None.

### Major

- **The headline claim that FSL "significantly outperforms traditional methods" is unsupported by the evidence on 2/3 datasets.** The abstract states this claim, but comparing RelationNet-attention (20-shot) against the only fair baseline CNN-attention-Few (same labeled data budget): on BCI 2a, FSL wins by ~10 points (72.6% vs. 62.8%); on BCI 2b, the gap is ~2 points (73.2% vs. 71.3%), within the standard deviations (±6.1 vs. ±6.1); on the Experimental dataset, FSL **underperforms** (68.2% vs. 69.2%). At lower shots, FSL underperforms CNN-attention-Few on 2/3 datasets. The comparison with CNN-attention-All (trained on all data) is irrelevant to the FSL claim—of course a model with full data access does better. The paper's framing groups the All model into the same table and then claims FSL superiority, when the actual evidence is mixed at best. This matters because it misrepresents what the results actually show.

- **No numerical comparison with the most relevant prior FSL work for EEG MI (An et al., 2020, 2023).** The paper cites An et al. as direct prior work applying FSL to the same BCI 2a and 2b datasets, yet never provides a head-to-head accuracy comparison. Section 4.4 mentions differences in approach (DA vs. fine-tuning, different data splits) but states "our model outperforms it" without any numbers. Without this comparison, the paper cannot establish that it advances over existing FSL methods for this specific task—the core contribution it claims to make.

- **The attention module's contribution is unsubstantiated by ablation.** The attention mechanism is presented as one of three "interconnected modules" and the paper claims it "improves prediction accuracy" (Section 3.2). Yet there is no ablation comparing RelationNet with vs. without attention, and no comparison with a vanilla Relation Network (Sung et al., 2018). The observed improvements over baselines could be entirely attributable to the RelationNet structure, not the attention. This matters because the attention module is a core claimed contribution.

### Minor

- **"DA Accuracy" is reported throughout Table 1 but never defined.** The paper never explains what data augmentation technique is applied, when it is applied (training only? training and testing?), or how it differs per dataset. This makes the DA accuracy column uninterpretable (Section 4.2, Table 1).

- **The evaluation protocol description is confusing.** Section 4.2 states "each training set included all experiments from a subject except the last one, which served as the validation set," which sounds like within-subject session-level splitting, but the cross-validation is described as subject-level (train on N-1 subjects, test on 1). These two descriptions are seemingly contradictory and need clarification—whether the within-subject split is for model selection within the meta-training phase.

- **Focal loss hyperparameters (α, γ) are never specified.** Section 3.3 introduces focal loss with parameters α and γ but gives no values. While the optimizer settings (Adam, lr=10⁻⁴, batch size 164) are stated, the focal loss parameters are missing.

- **Conclusion misleadingly attributes CNN-attention-All performance to the proposed FSL model.** Section 4.4 states "our model outperforms it [An et al.], achieving superior accuracy across all three datasets. Specifically, the CNN-attention-All model consistently demonstrated enhanced performance." The shift from "our model" to "CNN-attention-All" conflates the proposed FSL method with a non-FSL baseline trained on all data, which is misleading.

- **Small subject count in the experimental dataset.** With only 7 subjects and 7-fold CV, each fold tests on a single subject, meaning results are dominated by individual subject variability (high standard deviations, e.g., ±7.2% on BCI 2b 1-shot).

### Trivial
None.

## Nice-to-Haves

- Per-subject breakdown of results: with 7–9 subjects, reporting only mean±std obscures whether the method works reliably or succeeds on easy subjects and fails on hard ones.
- Statistical significance testing between RelationNet-attention and CNN-attention-Few, given that differences are often within one standard deviation.
- Attention heatmaps across multiple subjects (currently deferred to supplementary/future work with only a single subject).

## Removed Points

These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.

- **Harsh critic: "BCI 2a difficulty incorrectly attributed to 22 channels"** — This is factually wrong. The paper actually states (line 237): "BCI 2a does not include neurofeedback during recording, which makes the classification task more challenging **despite** having 22 channels." The paper attributes difficulty to the absence of neurofeedback, not to 22 channels. The harsh critic misread this.

- **Harsh critic: "Training time reduction is never measured"** — The contribution statement says "combines temporal convolutions and attention mechanisms to reduce training time," which is indeed vague and unsubstantiated. However, this is a minor presentation issue (vague claim in contribution list), not a structural flaw, and is captured by the broader overclaiming weakness.

- **Harsh critic: "Attention formula missing 1/√d scaling factor"** — While technically true that the paper uses S=QK^T without the standard 1/√d_k scaling from Vaswani (2017) and adds a non-standard tanh, this is a trivial implementation detail. The attention mechanism may work differently for EEG channel-wise attention (C×C matrix) than for token-wise attention in transformers, making the standard scaling inappropriate.

- **Harsh critic: "The architecture is not restricted to EEG data alone without evidence"** — This is a standard concluding remark about potential future applicability, not a central claim. While unsupported, it's a minor overreach in the conclusion, not a major flaw.

- **Harsh critic: Reproducibility concerns about hyperparameters** — Per the rules, missing hyperparameters (beyond α, γ which are already noted as a minor issue) and implementation details are removed as nitpicks about reproducibility.

- **Strength finder: "Interpretability through attention score visualization"** — Dropped because the evidence is extremely thin: only a single-subject heatmap in supplementary material, with the paper explicitly deferring broader results to "subsequent work" (Section 3.2). This does not constitute demonstrated interpretability.

- **Strength finder: "Use of focal loss for relation scoring"** — Dropped as it's a generic design choice without evidence that it matters more than standard cross-entropy would. No ablation comparing focal loss to CE is provided.

## Novel Insights

The paper reveals a tension common in applied FSL work: the FSL approach is most valuable precisely where it is hardest to validate. On BCI 2a (no neurofeedback, hardest setting), FSL clearly helps; on BCI 2b (with neurofeedback, easier setting), the simpler CNN-attention-Few baseline is already competitive. This suggests the FSL advantage may be dataset-dependent and tied to the inherent difficulty of the classification task, rather than a universal improvement—a nuance the paper misses by overclaiming universal superiority.

## Suggestions

- Reframe claims to match the evidence: replace "significantly outperforms traditional methods" with a nuanced statement reflecting that FSL shows clear benefits on BCI 2a but marginal or no improvement on the other datasets.
- Add an ablation removing the attention module from the RelationNet to isolate its contribution.
- Provide a numerical comparison with An et al. (2020, 2023) on BCI 2a and 2b, even if different data splits or augmentation strategies are used—report both under your setup and under a setup as close to theirs as feasible.
- Define "DA Accuracy" explicitly in the experimental section, including what augmentation technique is applied and when.

## Evaluation

**Originality**: Low. The framework combines existing components (Relation Network from Sung et al., 2018; self-attention from Vaswani, 2017; CNN embedding from Lashgari et al., 2021) without significant architectural novelty. The attention module lacks ablation to demonstrate it adds value beyond the RelationNet structure alone.

**Importance of research question**: High. Reducing calibration time for BCI via few-shot learning is a practically important problem with real clinical impact.

**Claim support**: Weak. The central claim of "significantly outperforms traditional methods" is contradicted on 2/3 datasets. The most important comparison (vs. An et al.) is absent. The attention contribution is unvalidated.

**Experimental soundness**: Moderate. Three datasets and cross-subject evaluation are appropriate, but missing ablations, missing key baselines, undefined metrics, and a confusing protocol description undermine rigor.

**Clarity**: Below average. The paper has confusing protocol descriptions, undefined terms (DA accuracy), and a conclusion that conflates the proposed model with the CNN-attention-All baseline.

**Value to community**: Moderate. The new dataset is a contribution, and the problem is important, but the current evidence does not convincingly advance FSL for EEG MI over prior work.

